---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import IrrCalculator from '@/components/calculators/IrrCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is IRR?',
    a: 'The internal rate of return is the yearly return an investment earns, based on its cash flows. Technically it’s the discount rate at which the net present value (NPV) of all cash flows is zero.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is IRR calculated?',
    a: 'There’s no simple formula. You find the rate r where −Investment + CF₁ ÷ (1 + r) + CF₂ ÷ (1 + r)² + … = 0 by trial and improvement. The calculator does this instantly. For $10,000 returning $3,000, $4,000, $4,000 and $3,000, the IRR is 14.90%.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a good IRR?',
    a: 'One above your hurdle rate: the return you need given the risk, often your cost of capital. Many businesses look for 10–20% or more, depending on the risk.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the payback period?',
    a: 'How long it takes for cash flows to repay the investment. In the example, $3,000 + $4,000 leaves $3,000 to recover in year 3, so payback is 2.75 years. Discounted payback does the same with present values, so it’s longer.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the limitations of IRR?',
    a: 'IRR assumes cash flows are reinvested at the IRR, can give more than one answer when cash flows switch between positive and negative several times, and ignores project size. Use it alongside NPV.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="irr-calculator"
  title="IRR Calculator – Internal Rate of Return & Payback"
  description="Free IRR calculator. Find the internal rate of return of an investment from yearly cash flows, with payback period, discounted payback and an NPV profile."
  intro="Find the internal rate of return of an investment from its yearly cash flows, compare it with your hurdle rate, and see the payback period."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-27"
>
  <IrrCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the IRR calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Enter the initial investment.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Enter the cash flow for each year.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Set your hurdle rate</strong> to see whether the IRR clears it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>IRR equation</h2>
    <div class="formula">0 = −Investment + CF₁ ÷ (1 + IRR) + CF₂ ÷ (1 + IRR)² + … + CFₙ ÷ (1 + IRR)ⁿ</div>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>
      Invest <strong>$10,000</strong> for $3,000, $4,000, $4,000 and $3,000 over four years. The IRR is <strong>14.90%</strong>: at that rate the present values add up to exactly
      $10,000. With a 10% hurdle rate, the project passes. The investment is paid back in <strong>2.75 years</strong> (3.47 years discounted at 10%).
    </p>

    <h2>Comparing projects</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>$10,000 project</th><th>Cash flows</th><th>IRR</th><th>NPV at 10%</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A</td><td>$3,000, $4,000, $4,000, $3,000</td><td>14.90%</td><td>$1,087.36</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B</td><td>$2,500 a year for 5 years</td><td>7.93%</td><td>−$523.03</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C</td><td>$16,000 in year 5 only</td><td>9.86%</td><td>−$65.26</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Project C returns the most cash ($16,000) but waits five years for it, so its IRR is lower than A’s. See the value in today’s money with the <a href="/npv-calculator">NPV calculator</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
